WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.230
Film Courage: Wendy, hearing what packaging
means, I think it sounds great if you’re

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:08.410
established but for a new writer, is that
something they should even be worried about,

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:09.670
thinking about or striving for?

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:14.590
Wendy Kram, Owner of Wendy’s LA4Hire: I
think that, again the first thing new writers

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:24.310
should concern themselves with is writing
a really, really good script.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:32.200
And I’m sure there are producers also who
come to Film Courage, I feel like when I hear

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:39.050
a story, if someone who…a writer that I’ve
been working with who is also a documentary

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:43.620
filmmaker who did a story and he showed me
a few clips and I’m like “Oh my gosh,

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:50.800
this is so moving, this is an amazing story
and immediately I could see that the character

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.210
whose story it was based on would be a great
role to attract a leading actor.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:01.710
So immediately I’m thinking big names.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:13.330
Now that then helps me promote it let’s
say if I call CAA and I create a good compelling

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:18.160
log line and I say it’s a great vehicle
for a leading actor and I might include some

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.220
prototypes and I go great send that over.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:25.530
Because everybody is usually (there are exceptions)
but are looking for something that is going

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:33.170
to attract a star whether it’s a female
leading actress or a male leading actor.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:39.700
And true stories very often is a really good
source to attract a leading actor.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:45.840
I mean I think if, the first priority is writing
a good story.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:53.140
I think with screenwriters and some screenwriters
that I know definitely think of a prototype

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:54.410
when they are writing it.

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:58.780
Like they might hear as I’m writing it I
see Marisa Tomei in the role and that can

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.130
help their writing process.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:08.010
I feel that anyone who gets too attached to
the outcome before you’ve done the process

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:13.340
is probably not a good way to go.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:17.950
In the case let’s say with producers or
you’re a writer and you don’t yet have

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:24.480
a script on a particular subject but you’ve
optioned a true story or maybe they’ve written

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:29.840
an article on yours in a magazine and it can
tell itself because there have been stories

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:32.980
optioned or turned into movies from newspaper
articles.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:36.930
I think BOYS DON’T CRY, I mean we know it
was a true story, the first time I became

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:43.330
aware of it I actually had read it in a magazine
and I wanted to option it.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:47.740
I tried to get the rights and sell it and
I was not able to do it.

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:54.730
At the time I was primarily focused on network
and it was a little too edgy for regular network.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:03:01.990
Anyway that did come from an article as well
as they said a true story.

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:08.540
ARGO was a true story and was also an article
which I think may have the jumping off point

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:14.140
for capturing the imagination of Clooney.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:24.091
And any time that you have something that’s
an article and if it’s a true story, you

00:03:24.091 --> 00:03:28.349
can sell true stories very often without having
a script.

00:03:28.349 --> 00:03:34.860
So if you’re a screenwriter, that’s something
to think about if there’s a true story that

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:40.870
you like and you might think “Oh, gee this
could be a great vehicle for any number of

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:42.730
young actresses.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:46.260
Or it offers a great role for a male lead
in their forties.”

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:53.070
So I think it’s always wise to think about
it but not the cart before the horse when

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:55.900
that becomes the dominant idea.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:02.660
But let’s say in the case of the writer
or producer has optioned the rights to a true

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:08.209
story then they would go out and trying to
package.

00:04:08.209 --> 00:04:14.860
So it would be about trying to get it in the
hands of a production company that has greater

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:21.359
access and leverage and ability to reach out
to agents and attach an actor, director and

00:04:21.359 --> 00:04:23.379
so on.

00:04:23.379 --> 00:04:27.770
Also, they can try to get to an agent.

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:37.610
I do coaching with those who are not represented
to help them get through the gatekeepers and

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:41.729
bypass certain industry protocols in order
to get their materials seen.

00:04:41.729 --> 00:04:50.440
There are ways that you can pitch that can
entice someone even if you don’t yet have

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:52.529
an agent.

00:04:52.529 --> 00:04:54.719
Does that answer the question about packaging?

00:04:54.719 --> 00:04:56.939
Film Courage: Yeah, I think so.

00:04:56.939 --> 00:05:03.319
Wendy: I would say generally (ideally) if
you can get it to a production company they

00:05:03.319 --> 00:05:09.580
are going to be in a better position to try
to attach those key elements.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:16.759
Film Courage: And so if someone says no, we
don’t take unsolicited material then doing

00:05:16.759 --> 00:05:23.279
it through someone like you can help kind
of procure that or also saying what if we

00:05:23.279 --> 00:05:24.279
do?

00:05:24.279 --> 00:05:31.080
Wendy: Well if they say we don’t take unsolicited
material, that the writer producer could say

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:35.219
“Can I sign a submission release form?

00:05:35.219 --> 00:05:36.680
Can you send that to me?”

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:40.599
And they should show it to their entertainment
attorney.

00:05:40.599 --> 00:05:46.020
A lot of writers don’t like to sign them
but it is an industry standard if you’re

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:55.969
not represented and that’s why you should
have an attorney take a look but it is fairly

00:05:55.969 --> 00:06:02.710
standard but I do understand why it’s a
Catch 22 because if they don’t do it they

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:04.750
might not be able to submit their project.

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:09.379
And I know for myself sometimes I’ve had
to do them and there are some (depending on

00:06:09.379 --> 00:06:13.240
the language) that I won’t sign and others
that I will.

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:16.759
Film Courage: And that’s where the attorney
[is needed] to review, you can’t just go

00:06:16.759 --> 00:06:17.759
online and…

00:06:17.759 --> 00:06:20.409
Wendy: And also just for me because I’m
not an entertainment attorney so I’m not

00:06:20.409 --> 00:06:24.250
just telling someone to go do this definitively.

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:30.509
They should go see an expert in that field
unless they themselves are experienced at

00:06:30.509 --> 00:06:32.270
reading those kinds of documents.

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:34.749
But generally, yes have an entertainment attorney
look.

